🔷 SOP Automation & Conversational Validation System — Refined Design Document (Agent-Ready)


📌 Context
- Organization: Bank operations / middle office.
- Current state: ~500 SOP documents stored on shared drives and Confluence. Documents are mostly text with some screenshots. No high-sensitivity content.
- Users: Operations staff (non-programmers), Team Leads, New Joiners, IT/Dev/Ops teams.
- Available internal LLMs: QWEN (253k token), Llama, Mistral, Gemma, etc., hosted internally.
- Constraint: All processing must remain inside bank network; no external LLM or cloud usage.

1. Problem statement (What we solve)
- Fragmented SOP corpus (500 documents) causes inconsistent answers, slow dispute resolution, high onboarding time, and manual validation effort.
- Goals:
  - Consolidate SOPs to 3–4 per team (AI-assisted, human-approved).
  - Provide instant, validated answers to staff queries with citations.
  - Allow staff to upload documents and validate them against chosen SOP sets/folders.
  - Maintain auditability, governance, and bank-grade security.

2. Key considerations (Requirements & constraints)
- Functional
  - Ingest SOPs from shared drives and Confluence (PDF/DOCX/HTML).
  - OCR screenshots and extract image text/step screenshots.
  - Semantic Q&A with source citations and confidence scores.
  - Upload-and-validate workflow (auto-select SOPs or user-select).
  - SOP consolidation assistant (cluster, summarize, propose masters).
  - Role-based access, approval workflows, versioning, audit logs.
- Non-functional
  - Latency target: chat responses < 3s median (pilot).
  - Availability: HA for critical services; backups and retention policy.
  - Scalability: support growth from 500 → 2000 SOPs.
  - Security: bank IAM/SSO, encryption (TLS + at-rest), audit trails.
  - Explainability: responses must include source snippets and provenance.
- Operational
  - IT builds and maintains the system; operations staff are end users only.
  - Start with pilot per team; incrementally onboard other teams.
- Constraints
  - No external data egress.
  - Internal LLMs available; staff have no programming keys.

3. Evaluated architectures (Quick summary)
- RAG-Based Chatbot (Chosen)
  - Components: Crawler, Preprocessor & OCR, Chunker, Embedding generator, Vector DB, Retriever, LLM synthesizer, Web UI, Admin console, Audit store.
  - Strengths: good handling of complex queries, low retrain cost, source-citation, modular.
- Fine-Tuned LLM with Full Context
  - Components: Fine-tune LLM on SOP corpus; load consolidated SOPs into context per query.
  - Strengths: consistent answers; Weaknesses: high compute for retraining, limited by context window growth.
- Knowledge Graph + Rule-Based System
  - Components: NLP extraction → Graph DB (Neo4j), rule engine, template responses.
  - Strengths: deterministic, low hallucination; Weaknesses: heavy manual modeling and maintenance.

4. Final decision (Architecture selected)
- Selected: RAG-Based Chatbot
- Why:
  - Balanced cost, infra, and performance.
  - Handles complex & novel queries via retrieval + LLM synthesis.
  - No continuous costly fine-tuning; incremental indexing is sufficient.
  - Fits internal LLM ecosystem and on-prem constraints.
- High-level diagram (textual)
  - Sources (Shared Drive, Confluence) → Crawler → Preprocessing (OCR, metadata) → Chunker → Embedding Generator → Vector DB → Retriever → LLM Orchestrator → API Layer → Web UI (Chat + Validation Workbench) & Admin Console → Audit & Analytics

5. Implementation pattern (Phases, components, APIs, and benefits)

Phase 0 — Planning & Compliance (2–3 weeks)
- Tasks: define pilot team, success metrics, data access approvals, compliance review, SSO/IAM plan.
- Deliverables: scope doc, compliance sign-off, sample dataset.

Phase 1 — Ingest & Index (3–4 weeks)
- Components:
  - Crawlers: Confluence API connector + file-share crawler.
  - Preprocessor: format normalization, OCR (Tesseract + vision model), metadata extraction.
  - Chunker: semantic chunk boundaries, provenance tags (doc id, page, section).
  - Storage: object store for raw/processed docs.
  - Embeddings: internal embedding model producing vectors.
  - Vector DB: ChromaDB/Weaviate/Milvus self-hosted.
- APIs:
  - POST /ingest/source {source_type, path, schedule}
  - GET /ingest/status/{job_id}
- Benefits:
  - Incremental updates, reproducible provenance, searchable corpus.

Phase 2 — Retrieval & LLM Integration (3–4 weeks)
- Components:
  - Retriever: semantic search + filters (team, date, author).
  - LLM Orchestrator: prompt templates requiring citations, multi-model fallback (QWEN primary).
  - Re-ranker & heuristics: boost recency, owner-approved SOPs.
- Key prompts (examples for agents):
  - System prompt: "Use provided source snippets. Cite each citation as [doc_id:page:section]. If uncertain, return 'INSUFFICIENT_INFO'."
  - Validation prompt: "Compare uploaded document fields with SOP rules; output JSON {compliance: bool, issues:[], citations:[]}"
- APIs:
  - POST /query {user_id, team, query, context_filters}
  - POST /validate {user_id, team, file, sop_set}
  - GET /citations/{response_id}
- Benefits:
  - Accurate answers with traceable sources, minimal hallucination.

Phase 3 — UI & Validation Workbench (4–6 weeks)
- Components:
  - Chat UI: conversation history, follow-ups, source snippets links.
  - Validation Workbench: drag/drop file upload, side-by-side comparison, auto-highlight deviations.
  - Consolidation Assistant: cluster visualization, auto-summaries, side-by-side diffs, approval UI.
- UX details for agents:
  - Provide structured response format (JSON + human text).
  - Actionable outputs: suggested master SOP draft, sections to merge, list of conflicts.
- Benefits:
  - Non-technical users can interact naturally; managers approve consolidated SOPs.

Phase 4 — Security, Governance & Testing (2–3 weeks)
- Activities:
  - SSO/IAM integration, RBAC, encryption, audit logs, data retention policy.
  - Model governance: hallucination detection, confidence thresholds, manual review flags.
  - Penetration tests, privacy review, UAT.
- Deliverables:
  - Security plan, audit configuration, risk register.

Phase 5 — Pilot & Rollout (4–6 weeks)
- Start: one team pilot.
- Measure: query accuracy, user satisfaction, time-to-resolution, consolidation progress.
- Iterate: retriever tuning, prompt refinements, onboarding.

6. Data models, APIs, and agent instructions (concise)

Data artifacts
- Document metadata: {doc_id, title, team, category, source, path, owner, last_updated, version}
- Chunk record: {chunk_id, doc_id, start_pos, end_pos, text, embedding_vector}
- Query record: {query_id, user_id, team, timestamp, query_text, retrieved_chunks[], response_text, citations[], confidence}
- Validation report: {report_id, uploader_id, file_meta, compared_sops[], issues[], score, citations, timestamp}

Core APIs (agent-ready)
- POST /api/v1/ingest {source, path, schedule, team}
- POST /api/v1/embeddings/generate {chunk_texts[]}
- POST /api/v1/query {user, team, query, max_results, filters}
- POST /api/v1/validate {user, team, file, sop_set}
- POST /api/v1/consolidate/propose {team, sop_ids[]}
- POST /api/v1/approve {user, master_sop_id, comments}
- GET /api/v1/audit/{query_id|report_id}

Agent instructions (for LLMs to start coding)
- Read this document as system context.
- Build modular microservices per phase (ingest, embedding, vector-db connector, retriever, llm-orchestrator, ui, admin).
- Implement APIs as specified; return structured JSON outputs for each endpoint.
- Enforce prompt templates requiring citations and confidence.
- Add unit tests and integration tests for ingestion → query → response flows.

7. Prompts & response format examples (for agents to enforce)
- User query prompt template (system-level):
  - "You are an operations assistant. Use only the provided source snippets. For each assertion, include citations in the format [doc_id:page:section]. If the answer is not directly supported, state INSufficient_INFO and list next steps."
- Validation output schema:
  - {
      "compliance": boolean,
      "score": 0..1,
      "issues": [{"field","expected","found","severity","citations":[]}],
      "recommendation": "text",
      "citations": [{"doc_id","section","snippet"}]
    }

8. Acceptance criteria & success metrics
- Functional
  - Chat returns relevant answer with at least one citation for 95% of queries in pilot sample.
  - Upload-and-validate returns structured report with identified deviations and related citations.
  - Consolidation assistant proposes master SOPs that a subject-matter expert (SME) accepts ≥70% of time in pilot.
- Performance & Reliability
  - Median chat latency < 3s (pilot).
  - System uptime ≥ 99.5% for core services.
- Adoption & Business
  - Time-to-resolution for SOP queries reduced by 50% for pilot team.
  - New joiner ramp time (first 30 days) reduced by measurable % (baseline vs pilot).
- Security & Compliance
  - No data egress; audit logs retained per retention policy.
  - RBAC enforced for all operations.

9. Minimal MVP feature list (for agent to prioritize)
- Ingest & index documents from shared drive + Confluence.
- Embeddings + vector DB setup with basic semantic search.
- Chat endpoint with LLM orchestration and citation enforcement.
- Upload-and-validate endpoint with structured report.
- Simple web UI: chat + file upload + view citations.
- Audit log for queries and validations.
- Consolidation assistant (basic clustering + proposed summary).

10. Team, infra & estimated timeline (MVP)
- Core team: 1 Architect, 2 Backend, 1 Frontend, 1 ML/AI engineer, 1 QA, 1 Product SME
- Infra: 2 mid-tier servers (API + retrieval), 1 GPU node for inference (use internal LLM infra), self-hosted Vector DB, object storage (MinIO)
- MVP timeline: ~12–16 weeks (pilot)

11. Risks & mitigations
- Hallucination risk → enforce citation-first prompts + confidence threshold + manual review for critical answers.
- Document freshness → incremental indexing + change detection.
- OCR errors → use hybrid OCR + manual verification for critical images.
- Knowledge drift → periodic retriever tuning and analytics-driven retraining of embeddings.

12. Next actions for agents / LLMs
- Step 1: Provision skeleton repos for services (ingest, embeddings, retriever, llm-orchestrator, api, ui).
- Step 2: Implement ingestion pipelines for a small sample subset (50 SOPs).
- Step 3: Implement embeddings + vector DB connector + a /query endpoint returning citations.
- Step 4: Build a simple chat UI to exercise the flow.
- Step 5: Run pilot, collect logs and tuning metrics, iterate prompts and retrieval parameters.
